00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:05.310
In the last video, you learned how to use a convolutional implementation of sliding

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:10.750
windows, and that's more computationally efficient, but it still has a problem of not

00:00:10.750 --> 00:00:14.390
quite outputting the most accurate bounding boxes.

00:00:14.390 --> 00:00:19.830
In this video, let's see how you can get your bounding box predictions to be more accurate.

00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:25.390
With sliding windows, you take this discrete set of locations and run the classifier through

00:00:25.390 --> 00:00:31.670
it, and in this case, none of the boxes really match up perfectly with the position of the

00:00:31.670 --> 00:00:32.950
car.

00:00:32.950 --> 00:00:36.110
So maybe that box was the best match.

00:00:36.110 --> 00:00:41.030
And also, it looks like in the ground truth, the perfect bounding box isn't even quite

00:00:41.030 --> 00:00:42.030
square.

00:00:42.030 --> 00:00:48.870
It actually has a slightly wider rectangle, slightly horizontal aspect ratio.

00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:54.030
So is there a way to get this algorithm to output more accurate bounding boxes?

00:00:54.030 --> 00:01:00.110
A good way to get this output more accurate bounding boxes is with the YOLO algorithm.

00:01:00.110 --> 00:01:06.550
YOLO stands for You Only Look Once, and is an algorithm due to Joseph Redmond, Santosh

00:01:06.550 --> 00:01:09.830
Divala, Raj Gershwick, and Ali Fakhadi.

00:01:09.830 --> 00:01:10.830
Here's what you do.

00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:14.350
Let's say you have an input image that is 100 by 100.

00:01:14.350 --> 00:01:19.390
You're going to place down a grid on this image, and for the purposes of illustration,

00:01:19.390 --> 00:01:24.430
I'm going to use a 3 by 3 grid, although in an actual implementation, you use a finer

00:01:24.430 --> 00:01:28.230
one, like maybe a 19 by 19 grid.

00:01:28.230 --> 00:01:33.790
And the basic idea is you're going to take the image classification and localization algorithm

00:01:33.790 --> 00:01:40.470
that you saw a few videos back, and apply it to each of the nine grids.

00:01:40.470 --> 00:01:45.230
And the basic idea is you're going to take the image localization, and the basic idea

00:01:45.230 --> 00:01:51.150
is you're going to take the image classification and localization algorithm that you saw in

00:01:51.150 --> 00:01:58.510
the first video of this week, and apply that to each of the nine grid cells of this image.

00:01:58.510 --> 00:02:04.030
So the more concrete, here's how you define the labels you use for training.

00:02:04.030 --> 00:02:11.550
So for each of the nine grid cells, you specify a label Y, where the label Y is this

00:02:12.550 --> 00:02:16.270
eight-dimensional vector, same as you saw previously.

00:02:16.270 --> 00:02:22.790
You'll first output PC01, depending on whether or not there's an image in that grid cell,

00:02:22.790 --> 00:02:32.110
and then BX, BY, BH, BW, to specify the bounding box if there is an image, if there is an object

00:02:32.110 --> 00:02:38.110
associated with that grid cell, and then say C1, C2, C3, if you're trying to recognize

00:02:38.110 --> 00:02:40.710
three classes not counting the background class.

00:02:40.710 --> 00:02:44.710
So you're trying to recognize pedestrians cause motorcycles in the background class,

00:02:44.710 --> 00:02:50.470
and C1, C2, C3 can be the pedestrian car and motorcycle classes.

00:02:50.470 --> 00:02:53.830
So in this image, we have nine grid cells.

00:02:53.830 --> 00:02:59.350
So you have a vector like this for each of the grid cells.

00:02:59.350 --> 00:03:04.150
So let's start to the upper left grid cell, this one up here.

00:03:04.150 --> 00:03:06.110
For that one, there is no object.

00:03:06.110 --> 00:03:14.830
So the label vector Y for the upper left grid cell would be zero, and then don't care for

00:03:14.830 --> 00:03:17.230
the rest of these.

00:03:17.230 --> 00:03:23.190
And the output label Y would be the same for this grid cell and this grid cell, and all

00:03:23.190 --> 00:03:27.910
the grid cells with nothing, with no interesting object in them.

00:03:27.910 --> 00:03:30.110
Now how about this grid cell?

00:03:30.110 --> 00:03:34.830
To give it a bit more detail, this object, to give it a bit more detail, this image

00:03:34.830 --> 00:03:40.270
has two objects, and what the YOLO algorithm does is it takes the midpoint of each of the

00:03:40.270 --> 00:03:45.830
two objects and assigns the object to the grid cell containing the midpoint.

00:03:45.830 --> 00:03:51.670
So the left car is assigned to this grid cell, and the car on the right, which has

00:03:51.670 --> 00:03:57.550
this midpoint, is assigned to this grid cell.

00:03:57.550 --> 00:04:03.190
And so even though the central grid cell has some parts of both cars, we'll pretend

00:04:03.190 --> 00:04:05.950
the central grid cell has no interesting object.

00:04:05.950 --> 00:04:11.670
So for the central grid cell, the class label Y also looks like this vector with no object,

00:04:11.670 --> 00:04:15.630
and as the first component, PC, and then the rest are don't cares.

00:04:15.630 --> 00:04:21.670
Whereas for this cell, this cell that's circled in green on the left, the target

00:04:21.670 --> 00:04:23.830
label Y would be as follows.

00:04:23.830 --> 00:04:31.030
There is an object, and then you write BXBY BHBW to specify the position of this

00:04:31.030 --> 00:04:38.190
bounding box, and then you have, let's see, if class 1 was a pedestrian, then that was

00:04:38.190 --> 00:04:44.150
0, class 2 was a car, that's 1, class 3 was a motorcycle, that's 0.

00:04:44.150 --> 00:04:47.670
And then similarly, for the grid cell on the right, because that does have an object

00:04:47.670 --> 00:04:55.630
in it, it will also have some vector like this as the target label corresponding to

00:04:55.630 --> 00:04:58.470
the grid cell on the right.

00:04:58.470 --> 00:05:05.070
So for each of these non-grid cells, you end up with a 8-dimensional output vector, and

00:05:05.070 --> 00:05:12.790
because you have 3x3 grid cells, you have 9 grid cells, the total volume of the output

00:05:12.790 --> 00:05:17.550
is going to be 3x3x8.

00:05:17.550 --> 00:05:25.430
So the target output is going to be 3x3x8 because you have 3x3 grid cells, and for each of the

00:05:25.910 --> 00:05:32.990
3x3 grid cells, you have a 8-dimensional Y vector.

00:05:32.990 --> 00:05:41.390
So the target output volume is 3x3x8, where for example, this 1x1x8 volume in the upper

00:05:41.390 --> 00:05:47.910
left corresponds to the target output vector for the upper left of the 9 grid cells.

00:05:47.910 --> 00:05:53.350
And so for each of the 3x3 positions, for each of these 9 grid cells, there's a corresponding

00:05:53.750 --> 00:05:59.270
8-dimensional target vector Y that you want in the output, some of which could be done

00:05:59.270 --> 00:06:01.110
here, if there's no object there.

00:06:01.110 --> 00:06:06.390
And that's why the total target output, the output label for this image is now itself

00:06:06.390 --> 00:06:08.910
a 3x3x8 volume.

00:06:08.910 --> 00:06:19.270
So now to train your neural network, the input is a 100x100x3, that's the input image, and

00:06:19.270 --> 00:06:27.790
then you have a usual conf net with conf layers, max pool layers, and so on.

00:06:27.790 --> 00:06:34.310
So that in the end, you would have this, you should choose the conf layers and the max pool

00:06:34.310 --> 00:06:42.590
layers and so on, so that this eventually maps to a 3x3x8 output volume.

00:06:42.590 --> 00:06:46.870
And so what you do is you have an input X, which is the input image like that, and you

00:06:46.870 --> 00:06:53.350
have these target labels Y, which are 3x3x8, and you use backpropagation to train the neural

00:06:53.350 --> 00:06:58.550
network to map from any input X to this type of output volume Y.

00:06:58.550 --> 00:07:04.870
So the advantage of this algorithm is that the neural network outputs precise bounding

00:07:04.870 --> 00:07:07.310
boxes as follows.

00:07:07.310 --> 00:07:12.110
So at test time, what you do is you feed in an input image X and run forward prop

00:07:12.110 --> 00:07:17.910
until you get this output Y, and then for each of the nine outputs, for each of the 3x3

00:07:17.910 --> 00:07:22.510
positions in which the output, you can then just read off one or zero.

00:07:22.510 --> 00:07:27.150
Is there an object associated with that one of the nine positions?

00:07:27.150 --> 00:07:30.510
And if there is an object, what object it is?

00:07:30.510 --> 00:07:36.350
And what is the bounding box for the object in that grid cell?

00:07:36.350 --> 00:07:39.830
And so long as you don't have more than one object in each grid cell, this algorithm

00:07:39.830 --> 00:07:41.910
should work okay.

00:07:41.910 --> 00:07:45.590
And the problem of having multiple objects within a grid cell is something we'll address

00:07:45.590 --> 00:07:47.030
later.

00:07:47.030 --> 00:07:52.030
But in practice, I've used a relatively small 3x3 grid.

00:07:52.030 --> 00:07:59.270
In practice, you might use a much finer grid, maybe 19x19, so you end up with 19x19x8.

00:07:59.270 --> 00:08:04.270
And that also makes your grid much finer and reduces the chance that there are multiple

00:08:04.270 --> 00:08:07.430
objects assigned to the same grid cell.

00:08:07.430 --> 00:08:12.670
And just as a reminder, the way you assign an object to grid cell is you look at the midpoint

00:08:12.670 --> 00:08:19.070
of an object, and then you assign that object to whichever one grid cell contains the midpoint

00:08:19.070 --> 00:08:20.070
of the object.

00:08:20.070 --> 00:08:25.750
So each object, even if the object spans multiple grid cells, that object is assigned only to

00:08:25.750 --> 00:08:31.750
one of the nine grid cells, or one of the 3x3 or one of the 19x19 grid cells.

00:08:31.750 --> 00:08:37.910
And with a 19x19 grid, the chance of a object of two midpoints of objects appearing in the

00:08:37.910 --> 00:08:41.910
same grid cell is just a bit smaller.

00:08:41.910 --> 00:08:43.390
So notice two things.

00:08:43.390 --> 00:08:48.830
First, this is a lot like the image classification and localization algorithm that we talked

00:08:48.830 --> 00:08:54.390
about in the first video of this week, in that it outputs the bounding box coordinates

00:08:54.390 --> 00:08:55.390
explicitly.

00:08:55.390 --> 00:09:00.910
And so this allows the neural network to output bounding boxes of any aspect ratio.

00:09:01.630 --> 00:09:07.870
As well as output much more precise coordinates that aren't just dictated by the stride size

00:09:07.870 --> 00:09:10.350
of your sliding windows classifier.

00:09:10.350 --> 00:09:14.150
And second, this is a convolutional implementation.

00:09:14.150 --> 00:09:20.470
And you're not implementing this algorithm nine times on the 3x3 grid, or if you're

00:09:20.470 --> 00:09:25.550
using a 19x19 grid, 19 squared is 361.

00:09:25.550 --> 00:09:30.870
So you're not running the same algorithm 361 times or 19 squared times.

00:09:30.870 --> 00:09:36.070
Instead, this is one single convolutional implementation where you use one confnet

00:09:36.070 --> 00:09:41.790
with a lot of shared computation between all the computations needed for all of

00:09:41.790 --> 00:09:46.830
your 3x3 or your 9x19 grid cells.

00:09:46.830 --> 00:09:49.230
So this is a pretty efficient algorithm.

00:09:49.230 --> 00:09:53.590
And in fact, one nice thing about the YOLO algorithm, which we should consider as

00:09:53.590 --> 00:09:57.470
popularity, is because this is a convolutional implementation.

00:09:57.470 --> 00:09:58.870
It actually runs very fast.

00:09:58.870 --> 00:10:02.430
So this works even for real time object detection.

00:10:02.430 --> 00:10:07.310
Now, before wrapping up, there's one more detail I want to share with you, which is

00:10:07.310 --> 00:10:12.590
how do you encode these bounding boxes, Bx, By, Bh, Bw?

00:10:12.590 --> 00:10:16.230
Let's discuss that on the next slide.

00:10:16.230 --> 00:10:21.430
So given these two cars, remember we have the 3x3 grid.

00:10:21.510 --> 00:10:25.070
Let's take the example of the car on the right.

00:10:25.070 --> 00:10:29.190
So in this grid cell, there is an object.

00:10:29.190 --> 00:10:32.350
And so the target label Y will be 1.

00:10:32.350 --> 00:10:34.390
That was PC is equal to 1.

00:10:34.390 --> 00:10:40.910
And then Bx, By, Bh, Bw, and then 0, 1, 0.

00:10:40.910 --> 00:10:43.950
So how do you specify the bounding box?

00:10:43.950 --> 00:10:48.750
In the YOLO algorithm, relative to this square, we're going to take the

00:10:48.750 --> 00:10:52.750
convention that the upper left point here is 0, 0.

00:10:52.750 --> 00:10:56.150
And this lower right point is 1, 1.

00:10:56.150 --> 00:11:02.990
So to specify the position of that midpoint, that orange dot, Bx might be,

00:11:02.990 --> 00:11:09.670
let's see, x looks like it's about 0.4, since it's maybe about 0.4 of the way to the right.

00:11:09.670 --> 00:11:16.430
And then y looks like that's maybe 0.3.

00:11:16.430 --> 00:11:24.030
And then the height of the bounding box is specified as a fraction of the overall width of this box.

00:11:24.030 --> 00:11:31.350
So the width of this red box is maybe 90% of that blue line.

00:11:31.350 --> 00:11:34.190
And so Bh, 0.5.

00:11:34.190 --> 00:11:42.110
And the height of this is maybe 1.5 of the overall height of the grid cell.

00:11:42.110 --> 00:11:46.550
So in that case, Bw would be, let's say, 0.9.

00:11:46.550 --> 00:11:53.790
So in other words, this Bx, By, Bh, Bw are specified relative to the grid cell.

00:11:53.790 --> 00:11:58.350
And so Bx and By, this has to be between 0 and 1.

00:11:58.350 --> 00:12:04.270
Because pretty much by definition, that orange dot is within the bounds of that grid cell it's assigned to.

00:12:04.270 --> 00:12:08.470
If it wasn't between 0 and 1, if it was outside the square,

00:12:08.470 --> 00:12:12.070
then what would have been assigned to a different grid cell.

00:12:12.070 --> 00:12:14.510
But these could be greater than 1.

00:12:14.510 --> 00:12:18.990
In particular, if you had a car where the bounding box was that,

00:12:18.990 --> 00:12:23.390
then the height and width of a bounding box, this could be greater than 1.

00:12:23.390 --> 00:12:27.110
So there are multiple ways of specifying the bounding boxes,

00:12:27.110 --> 00:12:30.790
but this would be one convention that's quite reasonable.

00:12:30.790 --> 00:12:35.710
Although if you read the YOLO research papers, the YOLO research line of work,

00:12:35.710 --> 00:12:39.030
there are other parameterizations that work even a little bit better.

00:12:39.030 --> 00:12:44.990
But I hope this gives one reasonable convention that should work okay.

00:12:44.990 --> 00:12:52.550
Although there are some more complicated parameterizations involving sigmoid functions to make sure this is between 0 and 1,

00:12:52.550 --> 00:12:57.270
and using an exponential parameterization to make sure that these are non-negative,

00:12:57.270 --> 00:13:01.510
since 0.9, 0.5, this has to be greater than or equal to 0.

00:13:01.510 --> 00:13:05.390
That there are some other more advanced parameterizations that work a little bit better.

00:13:05.390 --> 00:13:08.750
But the one you saw here should work okay.

